WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.187
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.187 --> 00:00:13.169
One of the topics that we think is
really interesting is what happens when

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:17.499
CEOs take a very controversial but
personal position on topics

00:00:17.499 --> 00:00:22.420
related to the environment or
society or even even political.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:26.980
And it gets a lot of attention, and
customers and employees see this and

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.955
it could have some either positive or
negative consequences on the company.

00:00:30.955 --> 00:00:34.340
We're finding it's about maybe 10% or

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.330
even less of the CEOs who are engaging
in activism in one form or another.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.150
So it's not like it's everybody doing it,
but

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:45.790
the ones that are doing it tend to
be very well known, very popular.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:48.004
You know, sometimes founders,

00:00:48.004 --> 00:00:53.071
sometimes people that just have had
a visionary impact on the corporation.

00:00:53.071 --> 00:00:58.527
[MUSIC]

00:00:58.527 --> 00:01:02.546
The types of things we're interested
in are activism by the CEO,

00:01:02.546 --> 00:01:05.551
along things that reflect
their personal view.

00:01:05.551 --> 00:01:10.554
We're not talking about positions
that the CEO takes, that you can

00:01:10.554 --> 00:01:16.190
imagine the position being taken as
one that makes the company better off.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:19.880
Something like immigration visas for
engineers.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.800
You can imagine companies that are tech
companies, that's a really good thing and

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.310
they may be controversial.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:29.350
But it's really something that
would help them as a company.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:34.543
So one thing that's interesting based
on our surveys, is there's certain

00:01:34.543 --> 00:01:40.100
topics that society seems to want people
to weigh in on and other topics not.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:45.450
So topics like healthcare, clean energy,

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.630
environments seem to be very,
very popular but things like abortion,

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.160
religion, politics, things like that.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.080
They tend to want the CEOs
of stay out of that.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:01.310
So something like I think we
all agree is really important

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.610
to talk about gun control.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.470
You know, it's a super controversial area,

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.800
especially depending on what
part of the country you're from.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.940
And so, you know,
do you want to take a take a stance or

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:18.700
potentially fund some
initiative on gun control?

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.335
Well, you know, you're going
to have a lot of employees and

00:02:21.335 --> 00:02:23.506
customers that will find
that to be a good thing and

00:02:23.506 --> 00:02:26.520
others that find that to be something
you ought of really stay out of.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:31.085
[MUSIC]

00:02:31.085 --> 00:02:35.040
So CEOs are much more visible,
there's a lot more eyes on them.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.950
So when CEOs are activists,

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.100
they take a very personal position
on a controversial topic.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.480
It shows up almost immediately.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.267
The velocity of social media is such
that you can go from nothing to

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:51.620
something pretty serious in
a matter of minutes or hours and

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.203
that can explode either in good ways or
bad ways.

00:02:55.203 --> 00:02:59.220
[MUSIC]

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:03.880
If you take a position that most customers
don't agree with, you could lose sales.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:09.800
Or if you take a position that's not
consistent with what employees want,

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.590
you could have a hard time attracting and
retaining employees.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:17.300
And one of the things we're doing now is
taking a look at what happens to sales and

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:21.100
purchases of companies typically
in the retail space but

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:26.920
where CEOs taken a very strong
position on a controversial topic.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.110
Did the sales go up or
down, things like that.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.060
If you take a stance on something,
you can start,

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:39.980
doing some arithmetic what
customers might we lose?

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.920
Or what customers might we
gain by doing these things?

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.940
Same thing on the employee side.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.470
There are some employees who
will be turned off by this and

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:50.970
maybe leave and
others will be attracted to it.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.490
The flip side of it is, and
we've done some surveys,

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:58.310
what you find is that about two-thirds of
the people want the CEOs take a leadership

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:01.860
position of some of these controversial
environmental social issues.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:06.670
It varies a bit, depending on your
political affiliation, or what generation

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:10.980
you're in, whether you're a boomer, or
a gen x'er, or millennial, or whatever.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.080
But if the cost and

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.350
benefits are something you want to
think about, it's hard to quantify.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:19.500
But it may be the case that
companies are wanting to maybe take

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:24.010
a hit in terms of the economics, but
actually do something really good for

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:25.900
the environment or other stakeholders.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:31.950
One thing that we find in the research
is some early indications

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:37.010
that consumers are willing to buy
more from companies that take

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:40.900
an activist position on
positions that they agree with.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.360
We find the flip side as well,
that they're likely to buy less from

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.920
companies that take the activist position
on something that they disagree with.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.167
So I mean, these positions can
shift customer purchases, and

00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:57.863
ultimately it affects
the bottom line of the company.

00:04:57.863 --> 00:05:02.199
[MUSIC]

00:05:02.199 --> 00:05:05.631
The one thing I think is really
key is that the board in

00:05:05.631 --> 00:05:08.848
the governance structure
of the organization.

00:05:08.848 --> 00:05:11.060
You have to anticipate these things and

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:13.940
they want to talk about it ahead
of time and not be blindsided.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:18.490
I think the smart boards are actually kind
of going through a bit of scenarios where

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.100
what will we do if.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.180
I think the boards really
need to think about,

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:28.046
do they want the CEO making personal
statements about these things or not.

00:05:28.046 --> 00:05:31.530
And one push back might be well,
it's the CEO saying it,

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.980
it's his personal opinion or
her personal opinion.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:38.100
But really, it's hard to
separate the CEO of the company

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.590
as a person from the company itself.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:43.510
So that can either be a good thing or
a bad thing.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:46.500
You want to put a kind
of constraints on them,

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.280
kind of lay that out in a formal way.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.800
And then if you take these positions,
you might want to think

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:57.740
about what's cost of benefits
of doing that for shareholders.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.152
So it's complicated.

00:05:59.152 --> 00:06:03.435
[MUSIC]

00:06:03.435 --> 00:06:08.360
So the role of board is tricky and
it's evolving, especially now.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:15.370
Companies have historically been in the US
at least focused on shareholder value.

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:18.625
More recently, some of the big
institutional investors like Larry Fink

00:06:18.625 --> 00:06:22.210
at BlackRock have sent
letters out to board say hey,

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.670
you have to take these other
stakeholders into account.

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:30.940
Customers, employees, the society,
environment and even the government.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:35.620
So I mean there's push to sort of
change from a strict shareholder focus.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:40.990
In that kind of setting you can imagine
CEOs advocating certain positions

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:45.240
that they view as better for society and
kind of embrace the stakeholders views.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:48.870
Where it's like let's really start paying
serious attention to environment or

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.990
workplace diversity or things like that.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:56.630
So I think it's going to be interesting
to see how the board's focus changes.

00:06:56.630 --> 00:07:00.784
And I think it's going to be interesting
to see how CEOs respond, but

00:07:00.784 --> 00:07:02.082
it's an evolution.

00:07:02.082 --> 00:07:06.987
[MUSIC]

00:07:06.987 --> 00:07:12.346
You could imagine at some point,
people demanding here are these

00:07:12.346 --> 00:07:17.940
topics that we feel strongly
about as customers and employees.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.050
What is your stance on this kind of thing?

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:23.620
There's a lot of things that would

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:27.769
probably make the environment
better off that are very costly.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:34.750
Where the value to shareholders
is probably not positive.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.900
That's where it becomes very,
very complicated.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.250
You're willing to make those investments,

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:43.030
just because it's the right thing
to do for those stakeholders.

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:46.970
And that whole thing has to be mapped out.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:51.770
It was certainly a lot more discussion
about stakeholder interest now.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:54.730
You know, people investing
in environmental, social and

00:07:54.730 --> 00:07:56.780
government sorts of funds.

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:59.220
And more money pouring into that,
you know, type of thing.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.900
You're going to have a whole
new generation of board members

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:05.800
that sort of understand this stuff,
grew up with it.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:08.530
And it think it's going to
change the discussion.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.441
It'll be interesting to see how
that plays out going forward.

00:08:11.441 --> 00:08:19.634
[MUSIC]

